{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_44","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n -i 'zero-copy|ordered append|spare capacity|P5|streaming.storage' AGENTS.md reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp --glob '*.report.md' --glob '*.prompt.md' | head -120\"","aggregated_output":"reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.review.prompt.md:11:1. **Does each workload measure what its budget names?** For P1–P5, P7, P8: the start and end marks, what is inside and outside the timed region (data generation, adapter setup, style and layout, paint, the frame after), whether \"complete\" is established or assumed, whether the accessibility layer is on, whether the build measured is the production build, whether the streaming clock is independent of the library's work, whether pointer latency is measured to the frame that shows the readout. Name every workload a budget needs that does not exist.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/scatter-missing.prompt.md:20:3. Streaming storage: `missingX` follows the zero-copy rules you just built (shared storage, offset on keep, nothing retained after retirement). Add it to the model in your property test in `test/data/streaming-storage.test.ts`.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data-store-3.prompt.md:9:- A one-row append on a 50,001-row line chart **replaced both numeric buffers despite spare capacity** (`src/data/store-buffers.ts` near line 52, the buffer fork).\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data-store-3.prompt.md:16:1. Reproduce both measurements as tests first (count buffer identity across appends with spare capacity; hold a `WeakRef` to dropped caller columns and assert collection after `global.gc()`, run with `--expose-gc` if the suite allows it, otherwise assert on the store's own references).\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data-store-3.prompt.md:17:2. Make `prepareAppend` / `commit` share storage between the displayed frame and the prepared one: a prepared frame is a view over the same growing buffers with its own length (and offset, for `keep`), and buffers are replaced only when capacity is exhausted (amortised doubling) or when the window has slid far enough to compact. The contract that a frame handed out earlier still reads its own rows must hold: read the frame and store contracts in `contracts.ts` (comments on `AlignedFrame`, `Series`, `RowSource`, `retire`) and say in your report exactly what a holder of an older frame may still read after a later commit, and until when. If the current contract makes zero-copy impossible, describe the smallest contract change and stop that part; do not edit `contracts.ts`.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.fix.report.md:25:| 8 create | fixed | `test/provenance.test.mjs` (trend checked value for value against `research/harness/src/data.js`), adapter spy test | Ran: P1/P5 use `--fixture trend`; the default is 20 blocks; Axylo's stream draw record no longer calls `describe()`. Not done: screenshot or trace validation of readiness. |\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/bench.fix.report.md:63:Declared tolerances and smallest worthwhile improvements per workload (screening requires `--tolerance` until then), a slope threshold for the P7 lifecycle gate, tile sizes for P5 and P8, and confirmation that \"20 runs\" means per configuration.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data-store-3.report.md:11:These measurements used one-row appends with spare capacity. The isolated stores already passed the original caller-column GC probe. Additional probes found that partially retained blocks could pin dropped objects and a caller’s backing buffer. Both are now collectible after retirement.\nreviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data-store-3.report.md:15:**Verification.** The [new suite](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/data/streaming-storage.test.ts) passed all 24 tests with `--expose-gc`, including 1,000 model runs per store and 2,000 appends per 600-row window. The million-point normalization test, scoped typecheck, and 39 Node controller tests passed. Repository-wide typechecking reported errors in browser acceptance files. Browser P3, the full P7 heap budget, and the uPlot attribution remain unverified.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data-store-3.report.md && cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data-store-3.prompt.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_45","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data-store-3.report.md && cat reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/data-store-3.prompt.md'","aggregated_output":"<!-- Author: Codex gpt-6.2-sol, effort xhigh (observed in the session rollout, thread 01a1ed01-7b40-7d93-8469-0f0dda830c3b). -->\n\nOrdinary in-order appends now share backing buffers between the current and prepared frames. `keep` advances the view’s offset, and storage grows or compacts as needed. I updated the [aligned store](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store.ts), [point and donut stores](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-unaligned.ts), and [row retention](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/src/data/store-rows.ts).\n\n| Store | Numeric buffers replaced per append | Typed-array bytes per appended row |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Line | 2 → 0 | 2,986,586 → 26 |\n| Scatter | 2 → 0 | 1,400,077 → 21 |\n| Donut | 1 → 0 | 10,572 → 30 |\n\nThese measurements used one-row appends with spare capacity. The isolated stores already passed the original caller-column GC probe. Additional probes found that partially retained blocks could pin dropped objects and a caller’s backing buffer. Both are now collectible after retirement.\n\n**Frame lifetime.** A committed frame keeps its normalized values, labels, and row indexes for as long as it is held. Its `source.rowAt()` can return its own retained rows until `retire()` reaches its revision, then returns `null`. A superseded, uncommitted preview keeps its normalized values and loses access to source rows. Spare bytes outside a frame’s typed-array views may change during preparation. The requested store signatures and shared contracts need no changes. Competing previews and writes to an already captured last-x slot can require separate storage. Grouped scatter and changes that require renormalization still use the full-update path.\n\n**Verification.** The [new suite](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/data/streaming-storage.test.ts) passed all 24 tests with `--expose-gc`, including 1,000 model runs per store and 2,000 appends per 600-row window. The million-point normalization test, scoped typecheck, and 39 Node controller tests passed. Repository-wide typechecking reported errors in browser acceptance files. Browser P3, the full P7 heap budget, and the uPlot attribution remain unverified.\n\nThe requested data run ended with one existing failure. Its assertion compares the entire backing buffer, including the spare bytes written during preparation. I left it intact.\n\n```text\nTest Files  1 failed | 4 passed (5)\n     Tests  1 failed | 125 passed (126)\n  Start at  06:07:07\n  Duration  2.51s (tests 93%, import 4%, transform 2%)\n```# Work package: the streaming store must append without copying\n\nRead `AGENTS.md` first; its rules apply. You own `packages/axylo/src/data/**` and `packages/axylo/test/data/**` and nothing else. Do not commit. You wrote this module (work packages `data`, `data.fix`, `data-store-2` in `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/`).\n\n## The defect\n\nAn independent review of the chart controller (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.controller.review.md`, finding 11; read it) measured, in Node:\n\n- A one-row append on a 50,001-row line chart **replaced both numeric buffers despite spare capacity** (`src/data/store-buffers.ts` near line 52, the buffer fork).\n- After `keep: 2`, and after an explicit data replacement, **the original caller columns stayed reachable** until an unrelated change rebuilt a cache.\n\n`docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md` section 2.2, the frame ownership comments in `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and the hot-path rule in `AGENTS.md` require appends to be incremental: cost proportional to the rows appended, not to the rows retained, and no reference kept to data the window has dropped. Budget P3 (spec section 5: 2 ms per update on a 600-point window) and P7 (memory) depend on it. The profile of the first benchmark pointed at this module for most of the gap to uPlot, though that attribution is not yet established.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Reproduce both measurements as tests first (count buffer identity across appends with spare capacity; hold a `WeakRef` to dropped caller columns and assert collection after `global.gc()`, run with `--expose-gc` if the suite allows it, otherwise assert on the store's own references).\n2. Make `prepareAppend` / `commit` share storage between the displayed frame and the prepared one: a prepared frame is a view over the same growing buffers with its own length (and offset, for `keep`), and buffers are replaced only when capacity is exhausted (amortised doubling) or when the window has slid far enough to compact. The contract that a frame handed out earlier still reads its own rows must hold: read the frame and store contracts in `contracts.ts` (comments on `AlignedFrame`, `Series`, `RowSource`, `retire`) and say in your report exactly what a holder of an older frame may still read after a later commit, and until when. If the current contract makes zero-copy impossible, describe the smallest contract change and stop that part; do not edit `contracts.ts`.\n3. After `keep` slides the window, or after `replace`, nothing in the store may reference dropped caller rows or columns once the older revision is retired.\n4. A property test (fast-check, at least 1,000 runs): for random sequences of append / keep / replace / reencode / retire, every live frame reads exactly the rows a naive array-of-rows model says it should, and the bytes allocated per append are bounded by a constant times the rows appended plus amortised growth (count allocations through a wrapper around the typed-array constructors the store uses, not by timing).\n5. The point store and the donut store: same checks.\n\nThe chart controller (`packages/axylo/src/core/controller.ts`) is being changed by its author at the same time to keep an explicit displayed snapshot and to retire revisions that were never displayed; do not edit it, but keep `retire`, `prepareAppend`, `prepareReplace`, `prepareReencode`, `fromFrame` and `retainedRowCount` source-compatible, and list any behavioural change a caller must know.\n\nRun `timeout 300 npx vitest run packages/axylo/test/data`. You cannot launch a browser. Never leave a process running. One existing test, \"borrows one million ascending typed values with a warmed median under 100 ms\", has failed intermittently on this loaded machine (125 ms once); do not change its threshold; if you can make the path faster, do.\n\nReport: what you built, the before and after counts (buffers replaced per append, bytes allocated per appended row), the last lines of the test run, what a holder of an older frame may rely on, any contract change you need, and what you could not verify.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
